The frozen turn count T

The delegation condition has a horizon term, T, and T is the number of hub turns after the first worker artifact. Every use of the condition therefore inherits whatever the turn count was at the moment it was taken, and the hub's message store does not hold still. The store compacts: superseded turns are evicted and rewritten, so a live count of its rows walks. On 14 October 2026 the same query returned 1,046 rows against the frozen 343. The store underneath the query had moved, and any reader who re-ran the query that day would have measured the walk rather than the record. This section sets out how the count is pinned, what the pinned object contains, where its interpretation stops, and how a reader can check all of it without touching the live store.

The object of record is an archive, data/t_freeze_2026-10-09T0416.csv. It holds 343 rows, each carrying a row id and a timestamp and nothing else. The archive was taken at commit cd46a7e, authored 2026-10-09 04:16 +04, which is 00:16 UTC on the same date; the local time of the freeze is visible in the filename. Its SHA-256 is 5572cf45f6c551739a4e7ed5afabebaf45f40deae28c27b85f0eafcd55eb4258. That hash, not the live store, is what the record's arithmetic leans on. A reader who holds the file and the hash holds the exact input the record used, whatever the store has done since, and the verification is mechanical: scripts/verify_t.sh recounts the archive and checks the hash, so a corrupted or silently edited copy fails loudly instead of quietly yielding a different count. Re-running the live query is not a reproduction of anything, because the store walks; the archive is the thing that can be reproduced.

The archive is not internally uniform, and the irregularity is the most instructive thing in it. Of the 343 rows, 122 share one of four second-resolution timestamps, all on 2026-10-08 and all in UTC: 48 rows at 08:46:41, 18 rows at 14:13:40, 26 rows at 15:21:55 and 30 rows at 17:42:43. Inside each burst the row ids step by two, so a burst occupies every other id slot across its range. The largest burst puts 48 rows inside a single second, and no model emits 48 turns in one second. The reading the record settles on is that these 122 rows are compaction rewrites: the store re-emitting rows it had already written, under fresh ids at a reused second-resolution timestamp. Two regularities make the reading tractable and the cleanup safe. First, the duplication is confined to the bursts: no timestamp in the archive is shared by two, three or four rows, so a row is either unique or belongs to one of the four bursts, with nothing in between. Second, each burst is taken to represent one underlying turn. scripts/dedup_t.sh removes the duplicates on exactly that rule and leaves 221 distinct turns, and the arithmetic closes: 343 rows minus the 122 burst rows is 221, so the deduplication accounts for every duplicated row and touches nothing else.

The rewrite reading is an inference, and the record treats it as one. The archive carries only id and timestamp. The store columns that would confirm the inference, role, active flag and finish reason, are not public, so no reader can open the store and see that the 122 rows are rewrites. What a reader can see is the arithmetic: burst sizes that no emission process explains, ids at a fixed stride inside each burst, and no duplication anywhere outside the four bursts. The inference is proportionate to that evidence, but it is evidence of structure, not of mechanism. Two further limits should be stated rather than glossed. The definition of T counts hub turns after the first worker artifact, and locating that boundary inside the store would need the role column, which the archive does not carry. The record also does not state, in so many words, which figure the horizon term binds, the frozen 343 rows or the 221 distinct turns left after deduplication; a reader reproducing the delegation arithmetic must take that binding from the delegation condition as published. Neither gap is closed here by guessing, because a supplement that guesses is less useful than one that marks the gap.

The freeze exists because an earlier practice failed. An anchor for the query, 277, was carried from a reviewer footnote without verification. Two reviewers re-ran the published query and got 266, and the episode is logged as incident INC-006. The refreeze to 343 followed. The point of the incident was not that either number was wrong in a way anyone could diagnose after the fact: no check existed that could say what the store held when the footnote was written, and by the time the query was re-run the store had already moved. What INC-006 established is a rule. A number carried without a check is not a basis for arithmetic, and the count of record is thereafter the archive plus its hash, not any live reading. The later observation of 1,046 rows against the frozen 343 is the rule confirming itself: the walk continued, the pin did not.

A reader who wants to reproduce the record works from the repository at commit cd46a7e, and the path is short. Take data/t_freeze_2026-10-09T0416.csv and compute its SHA-256; it must equal 5572cf45f6c551739a4e7ed5afabebaf45f40deae28c27b85f0eafcd55eb4258. Run scripts/verify_t.sh, which recounts the archive and re-checks the hash; the count must be 343. Run scripts/dedup_t.sh, which applies the four-burst rule; the result must be 221. Every step is deterministic, so any disagreement is itself a finding: it means the copy in hand is not the object of record, and the discrepancy should be reported against the archive rather than absorbed into the record's arithmetic. It is worth being exact about what the freeze does and does not guarantee. It guarantees the rows the store returned at the moment of the freeze, 00:16 UTC on 2026-10-09, and the internal structure of those rows, bursts included. It does not guarantee the rewrite mechanism, the location of the first worker artifact, or the choice between 343 and 221 as the figure the horizon term binds. Those limits are part of the record, stated here so that a reproduction knows where its certainty ends.

AB-S2-OK
